---
/**
 * The allergen statement.
 *
 * The most important page on this site. Someone reads it because they are
 * deciding whether they can safely eat this, or whether they can safely give
 * it to a child. So it is written to be accurate rather than reassuring, and
 * where the honest answer is "do not order this", it says that.
 *
 * The allergen list and the ingredient list are read out of the catalog, not
 * typed here, so this page and the printed label cannot drift apart. The
 * catalog schema refuses a product whose declared allergens do not cover its
 * declared ingredients, which is the check underneath this page.
 */
import LegalLayout from "@components/LegalLayout.astro";
import { business } from "@config/site";
import { getProduct, listPublishedProducts } from "@lib/catalog-source";
import { ALLERGENS } from "@lib/catalog";

const product = (await getProduct("gata")) ?? (await listPublishedProducts())[0] ?? null;

/* The catalog is the source. ALLERGENS is the fallback, and it is the same
   list in the same order, so a missing catalog cannot silently shorten it. */
const allergens = product?.allergens ?? [...ALLERGENS];
const ingredients = product?.ingredients ?? [];

const TOC = [
  { id: "contains", label: "What every tray contains" },
  { id: "cross-contact", label: "One kitchen, one set of trays" },
  { id: "ingredients", label: "The full ingredient list" },
  { id: "training", label: "Hakop's training" },
  { id: "severe", label: "If your allergy is severe" },
  { id: "label", label: "What is on the label" },
  { id: "changes", label: "If the list ever changes" },
  { id: "report", label: "If someone has a reaction" },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Allergens"
  description="Every tray of gata contains wheat, milk, egg, almonds, walnuts and sesame. It is baked in a home kitchen that handles all six, so cross contact is possible and there is no allergen free option."
  updated="2026-09-11"
  lede="Everything baked here contains six of the major allergens, and it is made in a kitchen that handles all six. This page says exactly what is in it and what cannot be promised."
  toc={TOC}
>
  <h2 id="contains">What every tray contains</h2>

  <p class="contains">Contains: {allergens.join(", ")}.</p>

  <p>
    That is not a warning about one variety. It is the only thing baked here,
    and every piece on every tray contains all of it. The sesame is scattered
    over the top by hand, so it is visible in the photographs and it is on
    every piece.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cross-contact">One kitchen, one set of trays</h2>

  <p>
    This is a home kitchen. One oven, one set of trays, one set of bowls and
    one counter. Everything listed above is handled in that kitchen, on the
    same surfaces, in the same week.
  </p>

  <div class="pullout">
    <p>
      <strong>This is not an allergen free facility and it cannot become
      one.</strong> There is no separate line, no separate equipment and no
      allergen free tray. Cross contact is possible on any piece.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    A tray cannot be made without one of its ingredients. There is one recipe
    and one product, so there is no walnut free version, no dairy free version
    and no wheat free version to ask for. If somebody offers to make you one,
    it is not this bakery.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ingredients">The full ingredient list</h2>

  <p>
    These are the ingredient names, in the order they are printed on the label,
    which the law fixes as heaviest first. The list on this page and the list
    on the label are the same list. If one changes, both change on the same
    day.
  </p>

  {
    ingredients.length > 0 ? (
      <ul class="ingredient-list">
        {ingredients.map((name) => (
          <li>{name}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    ) : (
      <p class="unsettled">
        <span class="unsettled-title">The ingredient list did not load.</span>{" "}
        Read it on the label on the tray, or write to {business.email} and ask.
        Do not assume this page is complete.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <p>
    How much of each, and everything about how it is made, is not published.
    Names are what the law requires and what an allergic customer needs. The
    rest is the family's.
  </p>

  <h2 id="training">Hakop's training</h2>

  <p>
    {business.owner} is ServSafe Food Handler certified and ServSafe Allergens
    certified. He is studying Culinary Arts Management at Cypress College,
    where his coursework has included Sanitation and Safety, and he has six
    years working in food service.
  </p>

  <p>
    That training is the reason this page is specific rather than vague. It is
    not a guarantee. A certificate does not turn a home kitchen into an
    allergen free one, and nobody should read it as meaning the risk above has
    been removed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="severe">If your allergy is severe</h2>

  <p>
    Plainly: if a trace of wheat, milk, egg, almond, walnut or sesame would
    send you to hospital, do not order this. A home kitchen cannot rule out
    cross contact, and this one is not going to claim otherwise in order to
    make a sale.
  </p>

  <p>If your allergy is less severe than that, some things worth knowing.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Every ingredient is listed above and on the label. Nothing is left off
      because it seemed minor.
    </li>
    <li>
      A tray is not individually wrapped by the piece, so pieces touch each
      other in the box.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you are buying for someone else, show them this page or the label
      before they eat any of it. Most people do not expect sesame.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you are unsure about something, write to{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${business.email}?subject=Allergen%20question`}>
        {business.email}
      </a>{" "}
      before you order. He will answer what he knows and he will not guess.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="label">What is on the label</h2>

  <p>
    Every tray carries the product name, the full ingredient list in label
    order, the allergen line above, the business name, the county that
    registered the kitchen, the registration number, and the statement that it
    was made in a home kitchen that is not inspected by the Department of
    Public Health.
  </p>

  <p>
    The label also carries a code that opens a page with the same ingredient
    and allergen text on it, so you can read it on a phone while holding the
    box rather than squinting at small print.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">If the list ever changes</h2>

  <p>
    If an ingredient changes, this page, the product page and the printed label
    change together, and the date at the top of this page changes with them.
    Trays already in someone's kitchen carry their own label, and that label is
    the authority for what is in that tray.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">If someone has a reaction</h2>

  <p>
    In an emergency, call 911 first. Nothing on this page is worth a delay.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once everybody is safe, write to{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${business.email}?subject=Allergic%20reaction`}>
      {business.email}
    </a>{" "}
    and say what happened, what was eaten and when. {business.owner} wants to
    know, he keeps the record, and it is how a mistake gets found rather than
    repeated.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>

<style>
  /* The allergen line is the sentence somebody came here to read. It gets
     the weight of a heading without being one, so the heading order stays
     honest for anybody navigating by headings. */
  .contains {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    padding: clamp(0.9rem, 3vw, 1.15rem);
    background-color: var(--color-paper);
    border: 2px solid var(--color-bad);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-crust);
  }

  .ingredient-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    padding-left: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 12rem), 1fr));
    gap: 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .ingredient-list li {
    padding-block: 0.45rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
